    glib::timeout_add_local(Duration::from_millis(1), move || {
        let Some(mut work) = state.borrow_mut().take() else { return Break; };
        ...
        *state.borrow_mut() = Some(work);
        let _ = &tick_state;  // tick_state keeps... wait, tick_state is moved into the timeout closure but never used to reschedule!
        Continue
    });
}
```

Bug: `tick_state` is captured but the timeout closure only continues the same timeout (ControlFlow::Continue) - so rescheduling works via ControlFlow::Continue on the same source. The tick_state clone is unnecessary - the same closure runs repeatedly with Continue. So I can simplify:

Actually with ControlFlow::Continue, the same timeout fires again - and `state` is captured by the closure. But I moved `state` into schedule... and tick_state is the second clone. On first call from timeout_add_local_once, both state and tick_state moved into schedule_bulk_recipe_tick, then into timeout_add_local closure. The closure captures `state` for use each tick - and on Continue, the same closure runs again needing state. That works if state is moved into the timeout closure. tick_state is also in the closure but unused except `let _ = &tick_state`.Wait - problem: after first chunk, we `*state.borrow_mut() = Some(work)` and return Continue. Same closure runs, takes state again. Good.

